string": "Convert bytestring columns (dtype.kind='S') to unicode (dtype.kind='U') assuming\nASCII encoding.\n\nInternally this changes string columns to represent each character\nin the string with a 4-byte UCS-4 equivalent, so it is inefficient\nfor memory but allows scripts to manipulate string arrays with\nnatur", "start_line": 2031, "end_line": 2045, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "convert_unicode_to_bytestring", "qualified_name": "convert_unicode_to_bytestring", "args": ["self", "python3_only"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert ASCII-only unicode columns (dtype.kind='U') to bytestring (dtype.kind='S').\n\nWhen exporting a unicode string array to a file, it may be desirable\nto encode unicode columns as bytestrings.  This routine takes\nadvantage of numpy automated conversion which works for strings that\nare pure ASCII.", "start_line": 2047, "end_line": 2060, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "keep_columns", "qualified_name": "keep_columns", "args": ["self", "names"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Keep only the columns specified (remove the others).\n\nParameters\n----------\nnames : list\n    A list containing the names of the columns to keep. All other\n    columns will be removed.\n\nExamples\n--------\nCreate a table with three columns 'a', 'b' and 'c'::\n\n    >>> t = Table([[1, 2, 3],[0.1, 0.2, 0.3", "start_line": 2062, "end_line": 2119, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "rename_column", "qualified_name": "rename_column", "args": ["self", "name", "new_name"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Rename a column.\n\nThis can also be done directly with by setting the ``name`` attribute\nfor a column::\n\n  table[name].name = new_name\n\nTODO: this won't work for mixins\n\nParameters\n----------\nname : str\n    The current name of the column.\nnew_name : str\n    The new name for the column\n\nExamples\n-----", "start_line": 2121, "end_line": 2163, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "add_row", "qualified_name": "add_row", "args": ["self", "vals", "mask"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Add a new row to the end of the table.\n\nThe ``vals`` argument can be:\n\nsequence (e.g. tuple or list)\n    Column values in the same order as table columns.\nmapping (e.g. dict)\n    Keys corresponding to column names.  Missing values will be\n    filled with np.zeros for the column dtype.\n`None`\n    All", "start_line": 2165, "end_line": 2216, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "insert_row", "qualified_name": "insert_row", "args": ["self", "index", "vals", "mask"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Add a new row before the given ``index`` position in the table.\n\nThe ``vals`` argument can be:\n\nsequence (e.g. tuple or list)\n    Column values in the same order as table columns.\nmapping (e.g. dict)\n    Keys corresponding to column names.  Missing values will be\n    filled with np.zeros for the col", "start_line": 2218, "end_line": 2356, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_replace_cols", "qualified_name": "_replace_cols", "args": ["self", "columns"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 2358, "end_line": 2365, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "argsort", "qualified_name": "argsort", "args": ["self", "keys", "kind"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Return the indices which would sort the table according to one or\nmore key columns.  This simply calls the `numpy.argsort` function on\nthe table with the ``order`` parameter set to ``keys``.\n\nParameters\n----------\nkeys : str or list of str\n    The column name(s) to order the table by\nkind : {'quicks", "start_line": 2367, "end_line": 2406, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "sort", "qualified_name": "sort", "args": ["self", "keys"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Sort the table according to one or more keys. This operates\non the existing table and does not return a new table.\n\nParameters\n----------\nkeys : str or list of str\n    The key(s) 