rtion and confidence interval in bins of a continuous\nvariable ``x``.\n\nGiven a set of datapoint pairs where the ``x`` values are\ncontinuously distributed and the ``success`` values are binomial\n(\"success / failure\" or \"true / false\"), place the pairs into\nbins according to ``x`` value ", "start_line": 278, "end_line": 452, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_check_poisson_conf_inputs", "qualified_name": "_check_poisson_conf_inputs", "args": ["sigma", "background", "conflevel", "name"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 455, "end_line": 464, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "poisson_conf_interval", "qualified_name": "poisson_conf_interval", "args": ["n", "interval", "sigma", "background", "conflevel"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Poisson parameter confidence interval given observed counts\n\nParameters\n----------\nn : int or numpy.ndarray\n    Number of counts (0 <= ``n``).\ninterval : {'root-n','root-n-0','pearson','sherpagehrels','frequentist-confidence', 'kraft-burrows-nousek'}, optional\n    Formula used for confidence interva", "start_line": 467, "end_line": 722, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "median_absolute_deviation", "qualified_name": "median_absolute_deviation", "args": ["data", "axis", "func", "ignore_nan"], "decorators": ["deprecated_renamed_argument('a', 'data', '2.0')"], "docstring": "Calculate the median absolute deviation (MAD).\n\nThe MAD is defined as ``median(abs(a - median(a)))``.\n\nParameters\n----------\ndata : array-like\n    Input array or object that can be converted to an array.\naxis : {int, sequence of int, None}, optional\n    Axis along which the MADs are computed.  The d", "start_line": 726, "end_line": 815, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "mad_std", "qualified_name": "mad_std", "args": ["data", "axis", "func", "ignore_nan"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Calculate a robust standard deviation using the `median absolute\ndeviation (MAD)\n<https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Median_absolute_deviation>`_.\n\nThe standard deviation estimator is given by:\n\n.. math::\n\n    \\sigma \\approx \\frac{\\textrm{MAD}}{\\Phi^{-1}(3/4)}\n        \\approx 1.4826 \\ \\textrm{MAD}\n\nwhere", "start_line": 818, "end_line": 875, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "signal_to_noise_oir_ccd", "qualified_name": "signal_to_noise_oir_ccd", "args": ["t", "source_eps", "sky_eps", "dark_eps", "rd", "npix", "gain"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Computes the signal to noise ratio for source being observed in the\noptical/IR using a CCD.\n\nParameters\n----------\nt : float or numpy.ndarray\n    CCD integration time in seconds\nsource_eps : float\n    Number of electrons (photons) or DN per second in the aperture from the\n    source. Note that this ", "start_line": 878, "end_line": 918, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "bootstrap", "qualified_name": "bootstrap", "args": ["data", "bootnum", "samples", "bootfunc"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Performs bootstrap resampling on numpy arrays.\n\nBootstrap resampling is used to understand confidence intervals of sample\nestimates. This function returns versions of the dataset resampled with\nreplacement (\"case bootstrapping\"). These can all be run through a function\nor statistic to produce a dist", "start_line": 921, "end_line": 1031, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_scipy_kraft_burrows_nousek", "qualified_name": "_scipy_kraft_burrows_nousek", "args": ["N", "B", "CL"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Upper limit on a poisson count rate\n\nThe implementation is based on Kraft, Burrows and Nousek\n`ApJ 374, 344 (1991) <http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1991ApJ...374..344K>`_.\nThe XMM-Newton upper limit server uses the same formalism.\n\nParameters\n----------\nN : int\n    Total observed count number\nB : floa", "start_line": 1034, "end_line": 1116, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_mpmath_kraft_burrows_nousek", "qualified_name": "_mpmath_kraft_burrows_nousek", "args": ["N", "B", "CL"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Upper limit on a poisson count rate\n\nThe implementation is based on Kraft, Burrows and Nousek in\n`ApJ 374, 344 (1991) <http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1991ApJ...374..344K>`_.\nThe XMM-Newton upper limit server used the same formalism.\n\nParameters\n----------\nN : int\n    Total observed count number\nB : f", "start_line": 1119, "end_line": 1193, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_kraft_burrows_nousek", "qualified_name": "_kraft_burrows_nousek", "args": ["N", "B", "CL"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Upper limit on a poisson count rate\n\nThe implementation is based on Kraft, Burrows and Nousek in\n`ApJ 374, 344 (1991) <http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1991ApJ...374..344K>`_.\nThe XMM-Newton upper limit server used the same formalism.\n\nParameters\n----------\nN : int\n    Total observed count number\nB : f", "start_line": 1196, "end_line": 1245, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "kuiper_false_positive_probability", "qualified_name": "kuiper_false_positive_probability", "args": ["D", "N"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Compute the false positive probability for the Kuiper statistic.\n\nUses the set of four formulas described in Paltani 2004; they report\nthe resulting function never underestimates the false positive\nprobability but can be a bit high in the N=40..50 range.\n(They quote a factor 1.5 at the 1e-7 level.)\n", "start_line": 1248, "end_line": 1325, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "kuiper", "qualified_name": "kuiper", "args": ["data", "cdf", "args"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Compute the Kuiper statistic.\n\nUse the Kuiper statistic version of the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test to\nfind the probability that a sample like ``data`` was drawn from the\ndistribution whose CDF is given as ``cdf``.\n\n.. warning::\n    This will not work correctly for distributions that are actually\n    dis", "start_line": 1328, "end_line": 1399, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "kuiper_two", "qualified_name": "kuiper_two", "args": ["data1", "data2"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Compute the Kuiper statistic to compare two samples.\n\nParameters\n----------\ndata1 : array-like\n    The first set of data values.\ndata2 : array-like\n    The second set of data values.\n\nReturns\n-------\nD : float\n    The raw test statistic.\nfpp : float\n    The probability of obtaining two samples this ", "start_line": 1402, "end_line": 1437, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "fold_intervals", "qualified_name": "fold_intervals", "args": ["intervals"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Fold the weighted intervals to the interval (0,1).\n\nConvert a list of intervals (ai, bi, wi) to a list of non-overlapping\nintervals covering (0,1). Each output interval has a weight equal\nto the sum of the wis of all the intervals that include it. All intervals\nare interpreted modulo 1, and weights ", "start_line": 1440, "end_line": 1489, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "cdf_from_intervals", "qualified_name": "cdf_from_intervals", "args": ["breaks", "totals"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Construct a callable piecewise-linear CDF from a pair of arrays.\n\nTake a pair of arrays in the format returned by fold_intervals and\nmake a callable cumulative distribution function on the interval\n(0,1).\n\nParameters\n----------\nbreaks : array of floats of length N\n    The boundaries of successive in", "start_line": 1492, "end_line": 1525, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "interval_overlap_length", "qualified_name": "interval_overlap_length", "args": ["i1", "i2"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Compute the length of overlap of two intervals.\n\nParameters\n----------\ni1, i2 : pairs of two floats\n    The two intervals.\n\nReturns\n-------\nl : float\n    The length of the overlap between the two intervals.", "start_line": 1528, "end_line": 1557, "is_async": f